§2. Deep History (From First Settlements Onward)

>>>

	* Pariah Myth - I like this one. I'd like to expand it. What are some real-world myths that fit the humans-turned-monsters in the wilderness as various fears, and how would they be known in the context of this world (eg. nomenclature wouldn't necessarily follow Earth-like ones)? How would the Mythborn evolve, considering that their live very oppositional lives and risk being hunted down throughout the ages? Are species that can't reproduce with humans/animals doomed to die off without reproductive partners, or is the fear of the unknown from these tribes cause random animals heard in the dark to anthropomorphize and mutate to fill in the empty role? Does Magic abhor a vaccum of beliefs, and seek to fill it out via evolutionary advantages (animals that are able to tap into Myth don't need as much food, can grow in power from fear, etc.)? Are they comparably weaker than Mantle-wearers or is Myth powerful enough that they need a party of knights/hunters/adventurers(?) with 2 active mantles and some support members to take them safely (two comparables that come to mind are the anime Claymore and the role playing game Mythic Bastionland, where difficulty vary wildly. I do not want to fall into generic adventuring tropes, but if the natural consequences of our worldbuilding decisions end up decontructing and rebuilding some tropes, I'd accept them)? I think there is a lot of potential with bugs (regular or like from Mushishi), beasts (more than animals, spirit beasts in cultivation novels, yokai in Japan), Fair Folks (of the more bestial kind, and different from the one we established the Fallen Travelers sometimes become) or boogeymen. And how does their existence and threat shaped settlements and society over the ages? The plot is unlikely to revolve around these kinds of myths, but their lore would inform a lot including gossip, laws, etiquette, beliefs, background events, character backstory, entertainment and a whole lot more. It's the kind of detail that reshape a world's structure, economy and culture, and is worth capturing early on.

