§4c. Common Mantle Archetypes

  * The Huntress - Would it make sense if the Mantle had some innate skill on horseback as well? How did hunters traditionally conduct hunts? Were they on foot guided by hounds to herd their game, or were they riding horses through the wilds? How does optimized hunting look when it comes to boars, bears, deers and more mythical creatures like swampdwellers or a vindictive dryad? Similarily, perhaps the Warlord or some others would extend the mantle to their horse where it makes sense. They might not be as apt as the Cavalier at using their mount like a weapon, but the horse might still be a part of their image.
    * Alternatively, The Huntress/Archer mantle could be a purely archery-based one. I'm less of a fan of this, as women in this world can be as military as men and don't have to limit themselves to a dedicated woman's sport, and also because strong Mantles are military assets first, sport prestige second. This mantle needs to have a layer of practicality to it.

§4.3 Relationship web: keeping the cast from orbiting Aveline

  * The 1rst son's Mantles need to include the Champion as mentioned elsewhere, and the Huntress at a level less than Aveline's before her death (Likely orange, since hers was yellow). This is a dead giveaway that he never stood a chance of roleplaying as her, and that the family needed to give that role to Gwion instead.
    * Him being a bully - Canon. He's not ill-meaning and he doesn't perceive himself as a bully, but he vents his frustration about the whole situation on Gwion. He is jealous and ashamed, while also relieved he doesn't have to play as a woman. Yet he'll never hear his own name again, and every time anyone else from then on calls him Gwion, it will be a reminder and a target for all these feelings. He softens with: distance, no longer having the scrutiny of being Aveline, the shared bleedings to feed their father throughout the Season, and with the realization that he deeply hurt Gwion if and when Gwion/Lleucu throws it back into his face.





